  "grammar": [
    { "id": "koevli", "title": "ko'év li: 'it hurts (to) me'", "concept": "dative", "body": [
      "Hebrew says **ko'év li ha-rósh** = (it) hurts to me the head. The body part is the subject; the verb agrees with it: **ko'évet li ha-béten** (béten is feminine).",
      "Same 'to me' construction as Latin **caput mihi dolet**. Arabic uses an object ending instead: rāsi byūjaʕni."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "כּוֹאֵב לִי הָרֹאשׁ וְיֵשׁ לִי חֹם.", "tr": "ko'év li ha-rósh ve-yésh li khom.", "en": "My head hurts and I have a fever." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "kl1", "prompt": "Say: My head hurts.", "answer": "כּוֹאֵב לִי הָרֹאשׁ.", "tr": "ko'év li ha-rósh." },
      { "id": "kl2", "prompt": "Say: My stomach hurts.", "answer": "כּוֹאֶבֶת לִי הַבֶּטֶן.", "tr": "ko'évet li ha-béten." },
      { "id": "kl3", "prompt": "Say: I've had a fever since yesterday.", "answer": "יֵשׁ לִי חֹם מֵאֶתְמוֹל.", "tr": "yesh li khom me-etmól." },
      { "id": "kl4", "prompt": "Ask a man: Where does it hurt you?", "answer": "אֵיפֹה כּוֹאֵב לְךָ?", "tr": "éfo ko'év lekhá?" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "negation", "title": "lo vs en", "body": [
      "**lo** negates verbs, adjectives and most things: aní lo yodé'a, ze lo rakhók.",
      "**en** is the negative of **yesh**: en li zman (I don't have time), en be'ayá (no problem). Formal Hebrew also uses en + pronoun ending for 'is not': **enéni** (I am not), but in speech people just say **aní lo**."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "אֲנִי לֹא חוֹלֶה, אֲבָל אֵין לִי כֹּחַ.", "tr": "aní lo kholé, avál en li kó'akh.", "en": "I'm not sick, but I have no energy." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "ng1", "prompt": "Say: I'm not sick.", "answer": "אֲנִי לֹא חוֹלֶה.", "tr": "aní lo kholé." },
      { "id": "ng2", "prompt": "Say: I don't have a fever.", "answer": "אֵין לִי חֹם.", "tr": "en li khom." },
      { "id": "ng3", "prompt": "Say: No problem.", "answer": "אֵין בְּעָיָה.", "tr": "en be'ayá." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "prepositions", "title": "Prepositions with endings", "body": [
      "Prepositions take the same endings as nouns: **im** → **ití** (with me), itkhá, itákh, itó, itá, itánu. **al** → **aláy** (on me), alékha… **el** → **eláy** (to me).",
      "Place words: **al** on · **le-yád** next to · **mul** opposite · **lifné** in front of / before · **akharé** after · **ben** between."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "בּוֹא אִתִּי לַשּׁוּק!", "tr": "bo ití la-shúk!", "en": "Come with me to the market!" }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "pp1", "prompt": "Say: with me", "answer": "אִתִּי", "tr": "ití" },
      { "id": "pp2", "prompt": "Say: The hotel is next to the market.", "answer": "הַמָּלוֹן לְיַד הַשּׁוּק.", "tr": "ha-malón le-yád ha-shúk." },
      { "id": "pp3", "prompt": "Say: The book is on the table.", "answer": "הַסֵּפֶר עַל הַשֻּׁלְחָן.", "tr": "ha-séfer al ha-shulkhán." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "imperative", "title": "Commands", "body": [
      "Short imperatives: **lekh!** go · **bo!** come · **kakh!** take · **tagíd!** say/tell. To a woman add **-i**: lekhí, bó'i, kkhí; to a group **-u**: lekhú, bó'u.",
      "In everyday speech Israelis often use the future as a softer command: **telékh yashár** (you'll go straight). Don't…! = **al** + future: **al telékh!** don't go!"
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "לֵךְ יָשָׁר, וְאַחַר כָּךְ שְׂמֹאלָה.", "tr": "lekh yashár, ve-akhár kakh smóla.", "en": "Go straight, then left." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "im1", "prompt": "Tell a man: Go straight.", "answer": "לֵךְ יָשָׁר.", "tr": "lekh yashár." },
      { "id": "im2", "prompt": "Tell a man: Take a taxi.", "answer": "קַח מוֹנִית.", "tr": "kakh monít." },
      { "id": "im3", "prompt": "Tell a man: Come here!", "answer": "בּוֹא הֵנָּה!", "tr": "bo héna!" },
      { "id": "im4", "prompt": "Tell a man: Don't go!", "answer": "אַל תֵּלֵךְ!", "tr": "al telékh!" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "future", "title": "Future tense: prefixes", "concept": "aspect", "body": [
      "The future adds prefixes (like the Arabic imperfect): **e-** I · **ti-** you (m), she · **ti-…-i** you (f) · **yi-** he · **ni-** we · **ti-…-u** you (pl) · **yi-…-u** they. katáv → **ekhtóv, tikhtóv, yikhtóv, nikhtóv**.",
      "Compare Arabic aktubu, taktubu, yaktubu, naktubu: the same Semitic system. halákh → **elékh, telékh, yelékh, nelékh**."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "מָחָר אֵלֵךְ לַסִּפְרִיָּה וְאֶכְתֹּב מִכְתָּב.", "tr": "makhár elékh la-sifriyá ve-ekhtóv mikhtáv.", "en": "Tomorrow I'll go to the library and write a letter." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "fu1", "prompt": "Say: I'll go to the market tomorrow.", "answer": "אֵלֵךְ לַשּׁוּק מָחָר.", "tr": "elékh la-shúk makhár." },
      { "id": "fu2", "prompt": "Say: We'll go together.", "answer": "נֵלֵךְ בְּיַחַד.", "tr": "nelékh be-yákhad." },
      { "id": "fu3", "prompt": "Say: I'll write a letter.", "answer": "אֶכְתֹּב מִכְתָּב.", "tr": "ekhtóv mikhtáv." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "modals", "title": "need / can / may + infinitive", "concept": "subjunctive", "body": [
      "**tsaríkh** (need), **yakhól** (can), **rotsé** (want), **efshár** (possible) + an infinitive in **l-**: **lilmód** to study, **lalékhet** to go, **lavó** to come, **lishtót** to drink.",
      "Levantine drops the b- in the same spot (lāzim rūḥ); Hebrew uses the infinitive."
    ], "examples": [
